# objective up. You derive the loss from the KL-regularised reward objective, implement it in numpy on a
# compact copy of the nanolm of T03-L03, check its gradient against finite differences, and then
# measure what it does: to the margin it was built to raise, to the likelihood of the answer you
# told it to prefer, to the length of what the model writes, and to its distance from the model
# it started as.
#
# **Time:** ~2.5 hours · **Runs on:** a laptop CPU, no GPU, no download, no network ·
# **Prerequisites:** T03-L03-nanolm-cpu-slice (the model and its hand-derived backward pass, which
# this notebook carries as a compact copy and does not load), T04-L02-chat-templates-and-masking
# (why only the response tokens count in the loss).
#
# By the end you will be able to:
#
# 1. Follow the derivation of the DPO loss from the KL-regularised reward objective, and check numerically that the
#    closed-form optimum maximises the objective and that the partition function cancels in a pair.
# 2. Implement the stable log-likelihood of a response, the DPO loss with its implicit reward
#    margins, its gradient coefficients, and the backward pass that re-weights per-sequence
#    log-likelihood gradients; verify the whole gradient against finite differences.
# 3. Measure, on held-out prompts, the implicit reward margin and pair accuracy of a DPO-trained
#    model, and count the pairs where the preferred answer became less likely while the margin rose.
